{"kind":"assert","text":"E4-B item 2 (ad086f45), from source at 73acdbe9:\n\nWhat `foldThrough` computes. `authorizationSource` asks `foldThrough` for one value: `revisions`, a map from item id to that item's revision just after the source entry (replica.ts:179-192, replica-own.ts:80). To get it, `foldThrough` replays entries 0 through the source's seq into a scratch `MemoryState` and reads `state.all()`. The `entry` half of its result is a cheap lookup (replica.ts:160-176). `authorizationSource` has one caller, `AuthorizationTransaction.source()` (authorization.ts:194). Its callers in turn are fill (:223, first fill only), permit (:254) and `item()` (:207), which retain loops over.\n\nDoes the replica hold the value? Only when the source entry is the head entry. The replica's live state (replica.ts:102) holds revisions as of the head, and `HeldEntries` keeps no revisions. Any later entry raises revisions (fold.ts:203), so the head state is wrong for an earlier source. In the measured run, the two sources were seq 1026 and 1027 against a head of 1027, and both paid a full fold.\n\nCan it be extended? Yes. The fold is a left fold over an append-only journal. The value at seq N depends only on entries 0..N, and no part needs a reorder or a later rewrite. The only invalidation is the refold from genesis (replica.ts:264-268), which must clear any kept value.\n\nThe three options, with their paths:\n(a) Read the live state when the source is the head. About 10 lines in replica.ts. A full fold returns as soon as any later entry lands.\n(b) Record revisions during the live fold, for the owner's own source entries. About 35-45 lines in replica.ts and scope-client.ts. It removes every fold. The risks: the refold reset, the memory each snapshot holds, and keeping the owner filter exact.\n(c) Keep the answer in a map keyed by the source entry hash, not by the head, and clear the map on refold. About 10 lines in replica.ts. The answer for a given source entry never changes, so a head-keyed cache would discard answers that are still right. Each distinct source still pays one fold, and every repeat becomes a map read wherever the head is.\n\nThe smallest change that removes the repeated fold is (c), keyed by entry hash and cleared on refold. (b) is the only one that removes the first fold too. The planner's head-keyed cache is a weaker form of (c). The 4096-entry run after your deployment END gives the growth of the first fold, which (c) still pays.","body":{"answer":"/private/tmp/artroom-e4b-fold-source-answer-20261116/ANSWER.md","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}